WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners episode 74. It's June 17th,

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:11.280
2026. Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it. Let's go!

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:16.720
Something big just happened with the software that runs your phone. Google released Android

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:23.660
17 today, along with an update called Wear OS 7 for smartwatches. The new version adds smoother

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:29.460
multitasking so you can juggle several apps without everything feeling cramped. It also improves

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.140
parental controls and adds stronger security tools that work quietly in the background.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:40.480
Most importantly, it brings Google's Gemini AI models deeper into the phone itself through a

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.060
simple update that arrives automatically. Think of your phone's operating system like the dashboard

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:53.720
of a car. In the past, that dashboard only showed basic gauges, but now it quietly adds helpful

00:00:53.720 --> 00:01:00.320
co-pilots that notice your habits and offer suggestions without being asked. Gemini works the same

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:05.240
way by learning how you switch between apps and then stepping in with smarter assistants across

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:11.360
everything you do. This matters because AI is no longer something you open in a separate app.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:17.320
It can now help organize your screen while you move between homework, messages, and videos.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:23.360
For students, that could mean faster ways to manage multiple tabs during a research project.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:29.760
You might notice the changes first in how smoothly apps resize or how suggested replies actually

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.200
fit the conversation you're having. Over time, this built-in help could change how you finish

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.240
everyday tasks without needing extra tools. If you have a recent pixel phone, you can check for the

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:46.480
update in your settings under System Updates. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.740
the deep dive where we look under the hood. You already know how your phone sometimes guesses

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:57.880
the next word while you are texting. Imagine that same auto-complete system now connects to everything

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:04.760
else on your device at once. First, it learns the words you type most often. Then it starts noticing

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:10.640
which apps you open together, like moving from messages straight to photos. Next, it watches

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.880
patterns across your whole day, such as checking the weather right after your alarm goes off.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:21.280
Finally, it uses all of those patterns to offer helpful actions without being asked,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.720
like suggesting a quick reply while you glance at your calendar. And that is basically what

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:33.020
Gemini is doing inside Android 17. Instead of only predicting text, it predicts what you might want

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.920
to do next across different parts of your device. So the next time someone mentions AI integration

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:44.180
in operating systems, you can tell them it is basically auto-complete for your whole phone.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:50.680
Not so scary, right? Now let's look at some cool stuff you can try yourself. Chat bots are getting

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:57.020
more competitive every month. Chat GPT still leads in total users according to recent tracking data.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:02.660
Other AI chat bots, like Gemini and Claude, are adding millions of new people every month

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:08.900
as reported by TechCrunch. Their steady growth shows that no single company owns the future of these

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:16.560
tools. Market share for Chat GPT has slipped below 50% for the first time, based on the same analysis.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.560
This shift highlights increasing options available to everyday users who want to compare different

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:29.080
systems side by side. You can test the competition right now by opening the Gemini app on your phone

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:34.980
or visiting the Gemini site on your browser. Ask it to plan a weekend activity built around your

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:39.840
favorite hobby and notice how the suggestions feel different from other chat bots you have tried.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.860
Here's another fun experiment that shows how creative these models are becoming. A new open

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:53.420
model called GLM 5.2 Max recently created a themed poem where certain letters disappear on purpose.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:59.740
The experiment was shared on X by researcher Ethan Mollick who noted the surprising creative

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:04.780
output. It followed unusual rules in a way that surprised people watching closely.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.140
The result demonstrates how newer models handle creative constraints more effectively than

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:16.380
earlier versions. Observers pointed out that the poem maintained coherence despite the deliberate

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:22.720
letter removals. This kind of test reveals progress in following complex instructions beyond

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:29.360
standard text generation. You can try something similar by opening any free chat bot and asking it to

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:35.520
write a short poem where every line hides one specific letter. Watch how the model bends the rules

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.180
while still making sense. Quick bits time starting with the hands-on preview of future laptops.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.800
Marquez Brownlee posted a video showing what it would feel like to add a touch screen to a MacBook

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:54.420
Pro. The clip was shared directly on X under his handle MKBHD. It offers viewers a realistic

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.900
demonstration of gestures and interactions that could appear in upcoming hardware.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:05.040
The experiment focuses on how touch input might integrate with existing trackpad and keyboard

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.900
controls. Brownlee's presentation gives a clear look at features that might appear in real products

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:17.340
later. Many viewers noted the natural feel of the simulated interface during everyday tasks like

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:23.540
browsing and editing. This kind of preview helps non-engineers visualize possible directions for

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:30.540
laptop design. In other news, a new collection of stories explores how armed forces around the world

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:37.000
are beginning to use AI models to help with decisions. The package was published by MIT technology

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:43.780
review and presented as an exclusive ebook. It examines specific examples of AI assisting

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:49.860
military planning and advisory roles across different countries. The material explains the shift

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:53.920
in plain language for anyone curious about where the technology is heading.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.640
Stories inside cover both current deployments and the questions raised by increased

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.900
reliance on these systems. The collection aims to make technical developments understandable

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:10.300
without requiring prior background knowledge. Readers can access the full set of articles

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:15.640
through the technology review website for further details on each case. That's it for today.

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:22.060
Remember, every AI expert started exactly where you are right now. If something we talked about

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:27.800
today made you curious, go try it. That's literally how learning works. Stay curious, keep

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:33.220
experimenting, and we'll see you tomorrow. And before you go, this show is part of the Nura Network,

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:39.820
a family of daily podcasts covering tech, science, markets, and more. If you enjoyed today's episode,

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:46.740
give SpaceX Daily a listen. The daily companion for following SpaceX as a public company from

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:52.140
Starship to the Stockticker, you can explore the whole lineup at NuraNetwork.com.

